---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/site';
import BuyButton from '../components/BuyButton.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="The themes, influences and making of The Very Last Superhero — a speculative novel of climate collapse, memory and care set in a near-future India."
  readingProgress
>
  <div class="shell section about">
    <header class="page-head stack">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>What we choose to remember</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose stack">
      <p class="lede">
        <em>The Very Last Superhero</em> is a fully illustrated speculative fiction novel set in a
        climate-collapsed India, blending AI, memory and resistance through the eyes of a
        fourteen-year-old girl.
      </p>

      <p>
        It begins after the era of superheroes rather than during it. In the 2040s and 50s, state-
        and corporate-backed heroes publicly tackled crime, pollution and dissent, and surveillance
        — welcomed at first — became ordinary. What followed was not a villain but a sequence: the
        Bangladesh flood, the Disclosures, an AI-authored National Curriculum, and the failure of
        four embankments on the Yamuna.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the ruins, heroism is not superpowers or spectacle. It is memory, care, and the courage
        to hold a community together when everything else has fallen apart. The Resistance is not an
        army. It is librarians, scientists, elders and teenagers running a filing system nobody in
        power can find.
      </p>

      <h2>Themes</h2>
      <ul class="themes">
        <li>Climate collapse and adaptation</li>
        <li>Memory, oral history and the archive</li>
        <li>AI, surveillance and technology ethics</li>
        <li>Youth agency and intergenerational knowledge</li>
        <li>Care economies and post-heroic politics</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
      <p>
        Two of the book's places are drawn from real ones the author has worked in, and she names
        them in the afterword. Nandini's transformed tea stall echoes the idea of a
        <em>sarai</em> — the resting places that once sheltered travellers on the trade routes — and
        more particularly the <a href="/act">CSDS Sarai</a> initiative of the early 2000s, which
        made liminal spaces where technology, community and critical thought could sit together
        outside institutional control. Mr Mehta's library in Ramnagar builds on her involvement with
        the <a href="/act">Bansa Community Library</a> in rural Uttar Pradesh, and what happens when
        books and ideas reach a place the formal system has given up on.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          These aren't just theoretical models or distant references — they are living initiatives
          that have shown how the architecture of resistance can be built, maintained, and passed
          forward through generations.
        </p>
        <cite>— <a href="/making">On the Making</a></cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>On the making of it</h2>
      <p>
        The afterword is printed in the book and reproduced here in full at
        <a href="/making">On the Making</a>. Its argument is that the superheroes were never the
        point: they were state mechanisms, designed for obedience and incapable of love, and the
        era that produced them grew out of post-truth politics, rewritten curricula and
        mythologised history rather than preceding it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sumati is not a chosen one. She is a child trying to understand what she has inherited, and
        her search for her father turns into a search for something older — shared memory,
        collective doubt, and the remnants of reason. She learns from relics, ruins, animals and
        ghosts. She knows from listening.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>
          The Firebird — etched on walls, drawn in soot — was their symbol. Not official. Not
          claimed. But everywhere. A reminder that collapse doesn't mean the end. It means the truth
          has outlived its suppressors.
        </p>
        <cite>— <a href="/making">On the Making</a></cite>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The illustrations were made with generative tools, manual curation and narrative revision.
        Some are memories the characters never see. Some are false. Some are true.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>They are not illustrations. They are records.</p>
        <cite>— <a href="/making">On the Making</a></cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Why waste-to-energy</h2>
      <p>
        The novel is dedicated in part to Dev, "for being the courageous father he is, battling
        Delhi's waste-to-energy plants all along." That fight is not fictional, and the book's
        argument — that the unglamorous, community-based work is the work that counts — comes
        directly out of it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can read about the ongoing campaign at
        <a href={site.campaign.url} rel="noopener">{site.campaign.label}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>The book</h2>
      <dl class="specs">
        <div><dt>Author &amp; illustrator</dt><dd>{site.author}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Publisher</dt><dd>{site.publisher}, New Delhi</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Edition</dt><dd>{site.edition}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>ISBN</dt><dd>{site.isbn}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Chapters</dt><dd>{site.chapters}, plus a prologue and an afterword</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p class="buy-row"><BuyButton /></p>

      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <p>
        Written and illustrated by {site.author}. Published by {site.publisher}. Site and interior
        artwork generated with AI image tools from written prompts, then selected and sequenced by
        hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Story, lore and artwork are licensed
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/" rel="license noopener"
          >CC BY-NC-ND 4.0</a
        >; the site's source code is MIT. Enquiries:
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    max-width: 52rem;
  }

  .page-head {
    margin-block-end: var(--space-l);
  }

  .about h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .themes {
    padding-inline-start: var(--space-m);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .themes li + li {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2xs);
  }

  .specs {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .specs > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(9rem, 14rem) 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    padding-block: var(--space-xs);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .specs dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .specs dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 30rem) {
    .specs > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-2xs);
    }
  }

  .buy-row {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-l);
  }

  cite {
    display: block;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xs);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }
</style>
